 This install applies to "Downwell", an unofficial native Amiga port of the
 2015 game by Moppin (Ojiro Fumoto), published by Devolver Digital.

 THIS IS AN EARLY RELEASE, A WORK IN PROGRESS: the first world only, no
 music yet, and partly optimized. It is not WHDLoad; the game runs
 from Workbench or from a Shell.


 What to expect:
 ---------------
 This version is the world 1 slice: the game from its start to the end of
 level 1-3, with the menus, gameplay, shops, side rooms, the pause menu, the
 results and the style select. It follows the Windows Steam release's own
 program and data, and is tested against them.

 - Music: not in this version (the sound effects play on Paula).
 - Speed: partly optimized. On the target machine gameplay averages about
   30 of its 50 updates a second: quiet stretches run at full speed, and the
   game slows down when the screen gets busy and in the side rooms. A faster
   processor helps. Full speed on a PAL Amiga is 50 updates a second,
   against the original's 60.
 - After level 1-3 the slice ends: world 2 is not in this version.

 Known problems:
 - The first level appears with a hard cut instead of the original's
   dithered reveal.
 - Gems about to vanish can show as white outlines.
 - With many shotgun pellets in the air at once (the shotgun and some
   upgrades), the game can stop: it has room for 32 bullets.

 Please report problems at https://github.com/Vesuri/downwell/issues


 Requirements:
 -------------
 The game needs a 68020 or better and Kickstart and Workbench 2.04 or
 newer. Its target machine, the one it is optimized and tested on, is an
 A1200 with 4 MB Fast memory. It needs about 1 MB of free Chip memory for
 its display and sound data, plus about 2.5 MB of further free memory (Fast
 or Chip) for everything else: about 3.5 MB in all.
 It refuses to start, and says why, when the memory is not there.

 Installing needs Installer 43 or newer, a 68020 or better (the installer's
 converter is a 68020 program), and about 4 MB free on the hard disk.

 The install does not include any part of the original game. You need your
 own copy of Downwell for Windows from Steam (app 360740), version 1.0.5.
 The installer reads these files from its Downwell drawer
 (Steam\steamapps\common\Downwell on the PC):

     data.win  Downwell.exe  language.ini
     amb1.ogg  sfxfalling.ogg  sfxbossfirsthit.ogg
     audiogroup1.dat  audiogroup2.dat  audiogroup3.dat
     audiogroup4.dat  audiogroup5.dat  audiogroup6.dat

 Copy the whole drawer to the Amiga (about 140 MB; the installer reads only
 the files above), or put it on a medium the Amiga can read. Every file is
 checked; other versions are refused.


 Installation:
 -------------
 Run the Install icon and choose a parent drawer: a Downwell drawer is
 created in it. Then select your Steam Downwell drawer. The installer
 converts the game's graphics, rooms and sounds from your files into
 Downwell's own data files in Downwell/data. This takes about an hour on
 an A1200's 14 MHz 68020 and about eight minutes on a 50 MHz 68030. If the
 conversion fails, the installer shows why and changes nothing.

 To update, select the same parent drawer and choose Keep. Use existing (the
 default) keeps the converted data; Replace converts your Steam files again.
 Remove deletes everything in the drawer except the saved game. The saved
 game (save.ini) is kept in every case.


 Playing:
 --------
 Start the Downwell icon, or from a Shell in the Downwell drawer:

     Downwell

 The game sets up the 8 KB of stack it needs itself.
 Joystick in port 2: left and right move, fire jumps and shoots (hold it to
 keep shooting while you fall), the second button cancels in the menus.
 Keyboard: X cancels; Escape or P pauses during play, and Escape quits
 elsewhere.


 Files:
 ------
 Downwell                the game
 data/                   its data, converted from your files by the
                         installer, and Downwell.fields and Downwell.columns
 save.ini                your saved game and options, written by the game

 DownwellInstallData, the converter the installer runs, decodes the Ogg
 Vorbis sounds with Xiph.Org's Tremor and libogg (BSD licences, see
 LICENSE.Tremor.txt and LICENSE.libogg.txt in the install archive); its
 file routines are under the LGPL (LICENSE.LGPL.txt).


